ace is the car roof.** The ground absorbs the sunlight, bakes in the sun, and turns that light into heat. The ground then warms up the air that is directly touching it. As you move further away from the "car roof" (the ground) and up into the sky, you are moving further away from the source of the heat. **4. The "Hairspray Can" Effect** There is one more major reason the sky is freezing, and it has to do with air pressure. Gravity pulls air down toward the ground, meaning the air down here is thick and compressed. As you go higher into the sky, there is less gravity pulling on the air, so the air spreads out and expands. In physics, **when a gas expands, it cools down.** You have probably felt this happen! If you ever spray a can of hairspray, compressed air, or spray paint for a few seconds, the metal can gets freezing cold in your hand. The gas inside is expanding as it escapes, which causes the temperature to drop rapidly. The exact same thing happens in the sky. As air rises away from the hot ground, it expands in the thinner atmosphere, causing it to literally refrigerate itself. **Summary:** Your car roof gets hot because solid objects absorb sunlight and turn it into heat. The air high in the sky is transparent, so it can't absorb sunlight. Instead, the sun heats the ground, and the ground heats the air. As you go higher, you get further away from the hot ground, and the air expands and cools down to freezing temperatures!
